     "content": "### Bottom Line Verdict\n\n**Do not accept this offer as-is, but do not walk away either.** You should **negotiate aggressively**.\n\nYou hold significant leverage:\n1. You were **sourced passively** and are happy in your current role.\n2. The current offer represents only a **~5% increase in Year 1 total compensation** ($552k vs. $526k).\n3. Your **guaranteed base salary actually decreases** ($235k vs. $240k).\n4. There is **no sign-on bonus** to offset switching risk or bridge unvested equity.\n\nLeaving a role where you have established credibility, strong performance, and high job satisfaction for essentially lateral compensation is not economically sound.\n\n---\n\n### Offer Breakdown & Critical Observations\n\n| Component | Current (Meta IC6) | Google Offer (L6) | Delta / Assessment |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | **-$5,000** (Posting range is $183k\u2013$271k) |\n| **Target Bonus** | $36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | **+$11,000** |\n| **Year 1 Equity** | ~$250,000 | $270,000 | **+$20,000** ($1.08M / 4 yrs) |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | N/A | $0 | Below market for passive FAANG-to-FAANG lateral |\n| **Year 1 Total Comp** | **~$526,000** | **$552,000** | **+4.9%** (Barely keeps up with a lateral move) |\n\n#### Hidden Risks in the Recruiter's Math:\n* **The \"Year 4 Cliff\":** The recruiter\u2019s projection of comp rising to ~$458k in equity by Year 4 relies heavily on hitting target refreshers every single year. Notice that once the initial $1.08M grant completes after Year 4, your equity drops back down to ~$250,000/year (base refresh level). Your Year 5 total compensation would revert to roughly **$532,000/year**\u2014virtually identical to what you earn today at Meta without any promotion.\n* **Refreshes Are Performance-Dependent:** Unlike an initial grant, refreshers fluctuate based on Google\u2019s macro performance multiplier, your ratings, and internal calibration changes.\n\n---\n\n### Market Calibration for Google L6 PM (SF / Bay Area)\n\n* **Base Salary Band:** $210k \u2013 $271k (Midpoint is around ~$240k\u2013$245k; $235k is on the lower side for an experienced IC6 lateral).\n* **Equity Band:** $1.0M \u2013 $1.4M over 4 years ($250k \u2013 $350k/yr).\n* **Sign-on Bonus:** $50,000 \u2013 $100,000+ is standard at L6 when pulling a strong performer from Meta/Apple/Amazon.\n* **Top-of-Band L6 Total Comp:** $600,000 \u2013 $650,000+.\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Counter-Offer Numbers\n\nPresent a counter-offer targeting a **Year 1 Total Comp of ~$660,000\u2013$680,000** (inclusive of sign-on):\n\n1. **Base Salary: Ask for $255,000**\n   * *Rationale:* You cannot take a base pay cut. Asking for $255k is within the approved range (up to $271k) and establishes parity.\n2. **Sign-on Bonus: Ask for $80,000 \u2013 $100,000**\n   * *Rationale:* Bridges the gap for walking away from Meta's upcoming vesting cycle and offsets the ramp-up risk.\n3. **Equity (GSUs): Ask for $1,280,000 over 4 years ($320,000/yr)**\n   * *Rationale:* A $200k increase on the 4-year grant ($50k/yr annualized) brings Year 1 guaranteed recurring comp up to a true FAANG-to-FAANG premium.\n\n---\n\n### Step-by-Step Negotiation Strategy & Script\n\nSchedule a call with the recruiter rather than negotiating entirely over email. Keep your tone enthusiastic about the scope/team, but transparent about the economics.\n\n#### Talking Points / Script:\n\n> *\"Thank you so much for putting this offer together. I\u2019ve really enjoyed getting to know the team and the problem space, and I\u2019m genuinely excited about what we could build together.*\n>\n> *That said, looking closely at the total compensation package, the current numbers don't make financial sense for me to leave Meta. As you know, I wasn't actively looking; I\u2019m in a great spot here with strong momentum, and the current offer represents a lower base salary ($235k vs. my current $240k) and a Year 1 total comp that is nearly identical to what I already make (~$526k vs. $552k).*\n>\n> *To make the transition compelling and offset leaving my current equity trajectory, here is what would get me to sign immediately:*\n>\n> * **Base Salary:** $255,000 *(well within the posted $271k band)*\n> * **Equity:** $1,280,000 over 4 years\n> * **Sign-on Bonus:** $80,000 to bridge the transition and forfeited near-term vesting\n>\n> *If we can get to these numbers, I\u2019m ready to sign and begin planning my start date.\"*\n\n---\n\n### When to Walk Away\n\n* **Walk Away If:** Google refuses to move on base salary, offers no sign-on bonus, and leaves total Year 1 compensation below **~$600,000**. Staying at Meta with your current standing and potential for promotion/refreshers is financially and professionally superior to taking a lateral move with zero risk premium.\n* **Accept If:** Google meets you halfway\u2014bringing base to **$245k\u2013$250k**, adding a **$50k\u2013$75k sign-on bonus**, and bumping equity above **$1.15M\u2013$1.2M** (Year 1 TC ~**$620k\u2013$650k**).",
